reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

Frozen journey requirements:
Step 0: seek #tab-account (initial)
Then step 1: ArrowRight -> expects focus moves to #tab-notifications and aria-selected true.
Observed failed at step 1? Actually they had two steps: first ArrowRight from account to notifications fails because focus didn't move to #tab-notifications (the assertion kind:"focus", selector "#tab-notifications" observed false). So pressing ArrowRight changed selection but focus remained on body? Let's see observed after state: After first ArrowRight, the HTML shows that #tab-account button now has aria-selected="false"? Wait look at "after" field: It includes the whole body HTML where #tab-account appears as having aria-selected="false". Indeed snippet: `<button class=\"nav-link\" id=\"tab-account\" ... aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">Account</button>` and #tab-notifications has aria-selected="true" and class active. However focus is still on body (selector body). They expected focus to be on #tab-notifications but got focus on body.

Thus issue: When moving via ArrowRight, the script updates aria-selected and active class but does not move focus to the newly activated tab. In bootstrap tab JS, keydown handler calls getNextActiveElement then Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show(); The show() method activates the tab (adds active class, sets aria-selected true, removes tabindex etc.) but does not focus the tab. According to ARIA authoring practices, when navigating tabs with arrow keys, focus should move to the selected tab.

So fix: In the _keydown method, after calling show() on the next tab, we should also focus that element (e.g., nextActiveElement.focus()). Or modify show() to focus. Minimal edit: In _keydown, after Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show(); add nextActiveElement.focus();

Check the code: In tab.js, _keydown method:

```
  _keydown(event) {
    if (!([ARROW_LEFT_KEY, ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_UP_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key))) {
      return
    }

    event.stopPropagation()// stopPropagation/preventDefault both added to support up/down keys without scrolling the page
    event.preventDefault()
    const isNext = [ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key)
    const nextActiveElement = getNextActiveElement(this._getChildren().filter(element => !isDisabled(element)), event.target, isNext, true)

    if (nextActiveElement) {
      Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show()
    }
  }
```

Add focus after show.

Now ensure we don't break anything else. Also note that show() already removes tabindex from the tab (sets tabindex removed?) Actually in _activate, after adding CLASS_NAME_ACTIVE, they call this._activate(getElementFromSelector(element)) for panel, then in complete they remove tabindex and set aria-selected true. Wait reading _activate:

```
  _activate(element, relatedElem) {
    if (!element) {
      return
    }

    element.classList.add(CLASS_NAME_ACTIVE)

    this._activate(getElementFromSelector(element)) // Search and activate/show the proper section

    const complete = () => {
      if (element.getAttribute('role') !== 'tab') {
        element.classList.add(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)
        return
      }

      element.removeAttribute('tabindex')
      element.setAttribute('aria-selected', true)
      this._toggleDropDown(element, true)
      EventHandler.trigger(element, EVENT_SHOWN, {
        relatedTarget: relatedElem
      })
    }

    this._queueCallback(complete, element, element.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_FADE))
  }
```

So after activation, tabindex is removed (so tab becomes focusable). But focus is not moved. So we need to focus.

Thus edit tab.js accordingly.

Now check other potential issues: The test also expects after second ArrowRight, focus moves to #tab-billing. Same fix will handle.

